  RED-CASE: an uncleared objection raised by the run's own orchestrator: never a deterministic approval either
  GREEN-CASE: the run orchestrator's own standing objection: refused an approval, routed to a stop that same surface can settle
  RED-CASE: an unreadable, empty or unrecognised arbiter class: read as the operator's, never as the one a woken model may clear
  RED-CASE: an objection carrying no arbiter class line at all: the operator-only stop, not the arbitrable one
  GREEN-CASE: an arbiter class left behind an empty objection: inert, and the deterministic approval is restored
  RED-CASE: a standing objection over a round whose own reviews reject: both facts in the record, not just the older one
  RED-CASE: an orchestrator's standing objection over a rejecting round: the round's rejection is composed for it too
  RED-CASE: a standing objection over an incomplete round: still the objection, and the shortfall is named in its record rather than implied
  RED-CASE: an objection detail missing the objection line or the remedy clause: caught here, because the answered page would be uncreditable
  GREEN-CASE: an operator's objection page: names the exact answer the relay credits, and only the operator's does
  GREEN-CASE: the objection detail, the page composed from it, and the clause the operator acts on: one composer, asserted at both ends
  RED-CASE: an authority record for another task, another instance, another objection, another candidate, other review evidence, none at all, or with a field nobody here knows: matched by none of it
  GREEN-CASE: the round a page presents: the candidate, the round's accepted envelopes and its pinned plan, each moving the digest on its own, and the candidate moving its own plain line too
  RED-CASE: a model-minted page quoting the objection and the remedy clause: no record, so no relay, however exactly its prose matches
  GREEN-CASE: the authority record: relayed once, spent by the consume, and worthless to the replay behind it
  RED-CASE: a page for another stop, another task, an orchestrator's objection or none at all: no authority record is minted for any of them
  GREEN-CASE: the driver's single page site is the one producer of an objection authority, gated on the shared predicate
  RED-CASE: notify --objection through the brokered command surface: refused, which is what makes the record evidence about the operator rather than about the model
