  GREEN-CASE: task create with & / backslash / placeholder-name titles: stored byte-for-byte, read back by both task show and doctor
  RED-CASE: task show against a zero-byte task file: refused, non-zero exit
  RED-CASE: task list with a damaged task file: a DAMAGED row, never a row of empty fields
  GREEN-CASE: task list with intact task files: id/status/title rows, unchanged
  GREEN-CASE: task show against an intact task file: printed in full, exit 0
  GREEN-CASE: a task nobody has ever arbitrated request-changes on: no unresolved_objection field is written
  RED-CASE: an operator request-changes: the objection is written onto the task, not left as a journal entry nobody rereads
  RED-CASE: an objection across a full rework round (new attempt, new verify, new review): still standing
  RED-CASE: task set on unresolved_objection: refused, and the objection still stands afterwards
  GREEN-CASE: orchid task arbitrate --result approve: the standing objection is cleared, and the clear is journaled
  RED-CASE: an objection with no recorded provenance: the arbiter's class is written on the task beside the text
  GREEN-CASE: the run orchestrator's own objection: recorded, survived a rework round, and settled by the same surface
  RED-CASE: a brokered arbitration over an operator's standing objection: refused at the verb, both results, nothing written
  RED-CASE: task set on unresolved_objection_by: refused, so an operator-only stop cannot be relabelled as a model-settleable one
  GREEN-CASE: an operator's own objection: settled by the operator, cleared, and both arbiters named in the journal
  RED-CASE: a hand-walked arbitration outcome (task advance out of arbitrating): refused, nothing moved, nothing journaled
  GREEN-CASE: the same arbitrating:rework edge through task arbitrate: taken, and the result recorded
  RED-CASE: a hand-walked merging on a task carrying an uncleared objection: refused, and the objection still stands
  GREEN-CASE: task advance blocked out of arbitrating: still legal, because stopping a task is not deciding it
  RED-CASE: a hand-walked arbitrating:done on an outcome=report archetype: refused too, not just the feature archetype's two edges
  GREEN-CASE: the report archetype: task arbitrate --result approve still reaches done, deriving it from the declared transitions
  RED-CASE: an outcome edge out of arbitrating on a custom archetype: refused there too, so the rule is not a property of the shipped transitions
  GREEN-CASE: a declared arbitrating:reviewing edge: still walkable by task advance, because it decides nothing about the candidate
  GREEN-CASE: task arbitrate on the custom archetype: still reaches merging, so the narrowed refusal closed nothing it was closing before
  RED-CASE: a brokered approve backed by no answer, an unanswered page, another answer, another task, or a page the model minted itself: refused every time
  GREEN-CASE: a brokered approve backed by the operator answer to this objection page: relayed, the objection cleared, the authority spent and the relay named in the journal
  RED-CASE: a stale operator answer against a superseded objection: refused, so an answer is bound to the objection it was given about
  GREEN-CASE: the operator answering the page for the objection that IS standing: relayed, so the binding refuses a replay without stranding the round
  RED-CASE: a byte-identical objection re-raised across a waived round: the earlier answer is refused, because the instance rotated where the text could not
  GREEN-CASE: the operator answering the page for the re-raised objection: relayed, cleared, spent — and the instance counter never rewinds
  GREEN-CASE: an objection longer than REVIEW_OBJECTION_MAX: stored within the cap, carried verbatim into the authority, and relayed
  RED-CASE: a question id drawn onto an answered page: redrawn, the earlier page untouched, and its approve authorising nothing for the objection standing now
  GREEN-CASE: the redrawn page: answered and relayed like any other, its authority spent, and both claims outliving the artifacts filed under them
  GREEN-CASE: a free question id: claimed on the first draw, and the page it names relays unchanged
  RED-CASE: a second accepted review landing after the operator answered: the same objection, the same instance, the same words, and an answer that authorises nothing
  GREEN-CASE: the page raised for the round as it stands: a different evidence digest, answered, relayed, spent — while the record it superseded is left exactly where it was
